{:edb/db-before-t 2856 :edb/db-after-t 2857 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536169 50 #inst "2026-10-09T17:57:31.937Z" 13194139536169 true] [17592186119683 1020 #edb/ref 17592186387007 13194139536169 true] [17592186119684 1020 #edb/ref 17592186387000 13194139536169 true] [17592186119684 1020 #edb/ref 17592186387014 13194139536169 true] [17592186119684 1020 #edb/ref 17592186387021 13194139536169 true] [17592186120287 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536169 true] [17592186120287 1100 "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(-1, - π)$ where $t = π$, given that $‖r^{′}(π)‖ = π$, and the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ to the curve is\n\n$T(t) = ⟨\\cos t,-\\sin t⟩$." 13194139536169 false] [17592186120287 1100 "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(-1,-\\pi )$ where $t=\\pi $, given that $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖=\\pi ,$ and the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ to the curve is\n\n$$\nT(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\n$$" 13194139536169 true] [17592186120287 1145 "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(\\pi )=⟨-1,-\\pi ⟩$,  since the point $P(-1,-\\pi )$ is at the tip of $r(\\pi ).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t)$, ${T}^{′}(\\pi )$ and $‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ⟩=⟨0,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\pi ) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖}{‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖}=\\frac{\\pi }{1}=\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=\\pi $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\pi ) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\pi )}{‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖}=⟨0,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\pi )+R(\\pi )N(\\pi ) & =⟨-1,-\\pi ⟩+\\pi ⟨0,1⟩=⟨-1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536169 true] [17592186387000 1017 #edb/ref 17592186387001 13194139536169 true] [17592186387000 1089 "q-132122" 13194139536169 true] [17592186387000 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139536169 true] [17592186387000 1100 "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ for the curve $r(t)=⟨t,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.$" 13194139536169 true] [17592186387000 1113 #uuid "97809c67-1610-5a64-b7c8-7cd83b34b3da" 13194139536169 true] [17592186387000 1145 "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),$ and $‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨1,-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-{sec}^{2}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨0,-{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ and ${r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,-2,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-2\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\sqrt{2})^{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536169 true] [17592186387001 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387002 13194139536169 true] [17592186387001 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387003 13194139536169 true] [17592186387001 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387004 13194139536169 true] [17592186387001 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387005 13194139536169 true] [17592186387001 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387006 13194139536169 true] [17592186387001 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536169 true] [17592186387001 1041 "selection" 13194139536169 true] [17592186387001 1104 #edb/ref 17592186387002 13194139536169 true] [17592186387001 1110 #uuid "b3d3c4c6-0b44-5b0d-a6f9-1147ba188c34" 13194139536169 true] [17592186387002 1034 "\\sqrt{2}" 13194139536169 true] [17592186387002 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536169 true] [17592186387002 1111 #uuid "ae552dad-8e26-5c41-a2b9-d8d9937ed6af" 13194139536169 true] [17592186387003 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139536169 true] [17592186387003 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536169 true] [17592186387003 1111 #uuid "a9733419-8d21-5608-8f7b-93f4e793048a" 13194139536169 true] [17592186387004 1034 "2" 13194139536169 true] [17592186387004 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536169 true] [17592186387004 1111 #uuid "c257cba8-19ab-5321-a41a-f812052cea8f" 13194139536169 true] [17592186387005 1034 "\\frac{1}{4}" 13194139536169 true] [17592186387005 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536169 true] [17592186387005 1111 #uuid "b3fd5867-0df2-518c-9728-b3678f36a31b" 13194139536169 true] [17592186387006 1034 "4" 13194139536169 true] [17592186387006 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536169 true] [17592186387006 1111 #uuid "ab76e0bd-e39f-5d1d-bd5f-d94612f118c4" 13194139536169 true] [17592186387007 1017 #edb/ref 17592186387008 13194139536169 true] [17592186387007 1089 "q-132208" 13194139536169 true] [17592186387007 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536169 true] [17592186387007 1100 "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(3,1)$ where $t=0,$ given that $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖=2,$ and the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ to the curve is\n\n$$\nT(t)=⟨-\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}⟩.\n$$" 13194139536169 true] [17592186387007 1113 #uuid "daba19b1-aa22-55bb-bccd-e7fd5bf20ab8" 13194139536169 true] [17592186387007 1145 "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(0)=⟨3,1⟩,$ since the point $P(3,1)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t)$, ${T}^{′}(0)$ and $‖{T}^{′}(0)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{3}}},\\,\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{3}}}⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{({0}^{2}+1)}^{3}}},\\,\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{{({0}^{2}+1)}^{3}}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=0$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=⟨-1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)+R(0)N(0) & =⟨3,1⟩+2⟨-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,1⟩+⟨-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536169 true] [17592186387008 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387009 13194139536169 true] [17592186387008 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387010 13194139536169 true] [17592186387008 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387011 13194139536169 true] [17592186387008 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387012 13194139536169 true] [17592186387008 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387013 13194139536169 true] [17592186387008 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536169 true] [17592186387008 1041 "selection" 13194139536169 true] [17592186387008 1104 #edb/ref 17592186387010 13194139536169 true] [17592186387008 1110 #uuid "e92735a5-d274-5884-b5a8-b12918f8a888" 13194139536169 true] [17592186387009 1034 "The center is $⟨3,3⟩$ and the radius is $2$" 13194139536169 true] [17592186387009 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536169 true] [17592186387009 1111 #uuid "b101f016-6b03-560e-9ceb-962053a6bd8c" 13194139536169 true] [17592186387010 1034 "The center is $⟨1,1⟩$ and the radius is $2$" 13194139536169 true] [17592186387010 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536169 true] [17592186387010 1111 #uuid "2b6643cd-7936-5c30-8c43-251437a3c93f" 13194139536169 true] [17592186387011 1034 "The center is $⟨3,2⟩$ and the radius is $1$" 13194139536169 true] [17592186387011 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536169 true] [17592186387011 1111 #uuid "7213c232-9aa2-5f34-af0b-314d43e800a5" 13194139536169 true] [17592186387012 1034 "The center is $⟨2,1⟩$ and the radius is $1$" 13194139536169 true] [17592186387012 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536169 true] [17592186387012 1111 #uuid "2f2a82f7-d40b-5359-8629-740cc96c9be4" 13194139536169 true] [17592186387013 1034 "The center is $⟨-1,1⟩$ and the radius is $2$" 13194139536169 true] [17592186387013 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536169 true] [17592186387013 1111 #uuid "b9ecf28d-3816-5163-a87a-b9ebf7ff3ec0" 13194139536169 true] [17592186387014 1017 #edb/ref 17592186387015 13194139536169 true] [17592186387014 1089 "q-98665" 13194139536169 true] [17592186387014 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139536169 true] [17592186387014 1100 "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ for the curve $r(t)=⟨{cos}^{3}⁡t,{sin}^{3}⁡t⟩.$" 13194139536169 true] [17592186387014 1113 #uuid "196994ed-bfc3-5fed-9e4d-698428010c6e" 13194139536169 true] [17592186387014 1145 "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t),$ ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ and $‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({cos}^{3}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{3}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨-3{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,3{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}),3{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}⟨-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t-3{cos}^{3}⁡t,\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t-3{sin}^{3}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}){sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-3{cos}^{3}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}){cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-3{sin}^{3}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}){(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}-3{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{3},6(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}){(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}-3{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}⟨1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ and ${r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})⟨-1,\\,1,\\,0⟩\\times (\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})⟨1,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})}^{2}\\cdot \\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\,-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}\\cdot \\left|\\begin{aligned}\\,-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{8}\\cdot 2\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{4}\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖}=\\frac{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3}}{\\,\\frac{9}{4}\\,}=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536169 true] [17592186387015 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387016 13194139536169 true] [17592186387015 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387017 13194139536169 true] [17592186387015 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387018 13194139536169 true] [17592186387015 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387019 13194139536169 true] [17592186387015 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387020 13194139536169 true] [17592186387015 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536169 true] [17592186387015 1041 "selection" 13194139536169 true] [17592186387015 1104 #edb/ref 17592186387018 13194139536169 true] [17592186387015 1110 #uuid "64aa0e09-0dd6-52a7-a88c-ddc291c107be" 13194139536169 true] [17592186387016 1034 "\\frac{9}{2}" 13194139536169 true] [17592186387016 1103 #edb/re